        "description": "<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"Maroon OpenAI logo on yellow background\" data-caption=\"\" data-portal-copyright=\"\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/08/STK149_AI_01.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\t\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">According to the <a href=\"https://www.ft.com/content/53082739-7714-4aae-9816-e55ab423cbee\"><em>Financial Times</em></a>, OpenAI disbanded its preparedness team at the end of last month. The job of the preparedness team was to assess if models posed serious risks and develop ways to mitigate those risks. (You know, like the possibility that it could <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/968988/openai-hugging-face-hack-ai\">go rogue</a> and hack another company.) According to <em>FT</em>, responsibility has instead been divided up for specific areas like bio and cyber, then moved into existing teams.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-parag",
